The ISO 9001 clause that requires quality objectives to be **measurable**, **established at relevant functions and levels**, and **backed by a plan to achieve them** is:

> **Clause 6.2: Quality objectives and planning to achieve them**

### Specifically, ISO 9001:2015 Clause 6.2 states:

- **6.2.1** – *The organization shall establish quality objectives at relevant functions, levels, and processes needed for the quality management system.*
- **6.2.2** – *Quality objectives shall be:*
  - **a)** Consistent with the quality policy;
  - **b)** **Measurable**;
  - **c)** Considered in relation to product and service conformity and the effectiveness of the QMS;
  - **d)** **Communicated**;
  - **e)** **Updated** as appropriate.

> **Additionally, 6.2.2 requires the organization to plan how to achieve its quality objectives**, which includes:
> - What will be done;
> - What resources will be required;
> - Who will be responsible;
> - When it will be completed;
> - How the results will be evaluated.

### So, your vague statement like “improve quality” fails to meet 6.2.2 because it is:
- Not **measurable** (no metric or target),
- Lacks **owners** (no assigned responsibility),
- Lacks **deadlines** (no timeline),
- And has no **plan** for achievement.

### ✅ Corrective Action:
Revise objectives to be SMART:
> *“Reduce customer returns due to manufacturing defects by 30% by December 31, 2024, led by the Production Manager, through implementation of enhanced inspection checkpoints and operator training.”*

This satisfies **Clause 6.2** and demonstrates compliance with ISO 9001:2015.

> **Key takeaway**: Clause 6.2 is the heart of effective quality objective management in ISO 9001.
